; Stage 15 W5c: `CStr` is FLOW-EXEMPT and a null-initialised `CStr` global must
; keep compiling.
;
; This fixture exists as a tripwire for the KNOWN, SEPARATE null-safety hole
; beside it, which W5c deliberately did not close and Stage 15 W6 since did:
; `defvar`'s global initializer is a constant renderer that never routes through
; `coerce-int-val`, so `pkind-flow-check` never ran and `(defvar g:ptr:Thing
; null)` compiled clean then segfaulted at runtime, while the identical *local*
; binding was correctly rejected.
;
; W6 added that pkind check to `defvar-init-ir`'s `null` branch on the `TY-PTR`
; path ONLY (see tests/fixtures/w6-defvar-null-*.nuc). `CStr` is the C `char*`
; FFI type, where a null pointer is ordinary and meaningful, and is grouped with
; `void*` outside the Phase-F non-null regime by context/conventions.md's
; string-type lattice. If this file ever fails to compile, that carve-out has
; been lost.
;
; Stage 15 W9 item 7 narrowed which carve-out that is, and the DIRECTION is the
; whole of it. A null into a `CStr` DESTINATION — every line below — is ordinary
; C and stays legal. A `CStr` SOURCE into a typed non-null destination is not,
; and no longer compiles: `CStr` carries no non-null contract of its own, so
; `(defvar g:ptr:T (as CStr null))` used to store a null in a slot whose type
; forbids it (tests/fixtures/w9-cstr-into-ref-*.nuc).
(defvar g-null:CStr null)
(defvar g-noinit:CStr)

(defn main ():i32
  (if (= g-null null) (return 0) (return 1)))
